 ## Overview
-Marching Squares is an algorithm for extracting a polygonal contour (isolines) from a two-dimensional scalar field by processing each grid cell independently [S2, S11]. It determines the topology of the isosurface intersection with a square cell based on the scalar values at the cell's four vertices relative to an isolevel [S2]. Unlike its 3D counterpart, Marching Cubes, which can produce ambiguous saddle points requiring additional heuristics, the 2D case on a square grid has no topological ambiguity for standard isolines, though it shares the same lookup-table and interpolation framework [S2, S11]. It is the 2D analog of [Marching Cubes](774-marching-cubes.md) [S2, S11].
+Marching Squares is an algorithm for extracting a polygonal contour (isolines) from a two-dimensional scalar field by processing each grid cell independently [S2, S11]. It determines the topology of the isosurface intersection with a square cell based on the scalar values at the cell's four vertices relative to an isolevel [S2]. Unlike its 3D counterpart, [Marching Cubes](774-marching-cubes.md), which can produce ambiguous saddle points requiring additional heuristics, the 2D case on a square grid shares the same lookup-table and interpolation framework but requires explicit resolution for saddle points (cases 5 and 10) using the cell center value [S2, S11].
 
